\details{
All free parameters enter the model through a log link, so the coefficients
returned by \code{\link[=coef]{coef()}} are on the log scale. The threshold is parameterised as
\code{b = A + exp(eta)}, which guarantees \code{b > A}; its coefficients therefore
describe the log distance between the threshold and the upper bound of the
start point. Use \code{coef(fit, type = "ratio")} for the exponentiated
coefficients, which are multiplicative effects rather than the parameters,
and \code{\link[=fitted.rdm_fit]{fitted.rdm_fit()}} for the trial-level parameters themselves.

A term inside \code{acc()} is estimated separately for each accumulator, so
\code{b = ~ acc(1)} gives one threshold distance per accumulator (a response
bias). Inside \code{acc()} the current accumulator is available as \code{.acc}, and a
term that refers to it is a single predictor instead: \code{acc(.acc == Word)} is
one coefficient that is 1 where the accumulator matches the word. See
\code{\link[=acc]{acc()}}.

The left-hand side of a random-effect term is built like the fixed part, so
\code{acc()} can be used there as well and the random effects can mirror the fixed
effects: with \code{v = ~ acc(Condition) + (acc(Condition) | id)} every
accumulator-and-condition effect of the fixed part has a random counterpart.
The accumulator variables \code{response} and \code{.acc} are available there too.
